examples.solution_text:
Split $4=2\times2$ and $169=13\times13$. Every leaf is prime.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black]
\path[use as bounding box] (-2.4,-0.3) rectangle (2.4,2.4);
\node (a) at (0,2) {$676$};
\node (b) at (-1.3,1) {$4$};\node (c) at (1.3,1) {$169$};
\node (d) at (-2,0) {$2$};\node (e) at (-0.6,0) {$2$};
\node (f) at (0.6,0) {$13$};\node (g) at (2,0) {$13$};
\draw (a)--(b);\draw (a)--(c);\draw (b)--(d);\draw (b)--(e);\draw (c)--(f);\draw (c)--(g);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$676=2\times2\times13\times13=(2\times13)\times(2\times13)$.
One factor from each equal pair gives $\sqrt{676}=2\times13=26$.

examples.question_text:
A square has area $900$ cm$^2$, and $900=2^2\times3^2\times5^2$.
Find its side length.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\path (1.5,1.5) node {$900\text{ cm}^2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Halve each prime index to find the side.
$\sqrt{900}=2\times3\times5=30$.
Each side is $30$ cm long.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3);
\path (1.5,1.5) node {$900\text{ cm}^2$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$30\text{ cm}$};
\path (0,1.5) node[left] {$30\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
